WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.506 --> 00:00:08.709
So, it's my pleasure to
introduce our first speaker,

00:00:08.709 --> 00:00:10.377
Dr. Robert Grossman.

00:00:10.377 --> 00:00:13.914
He is the Director of the Center
for Data Intensive Sciences,

00:00:13.914 --> 00:00:17.084
and a Professor in the Division
of Biological Sciences at the

00:00:17.084 --> 00:00:18.785
University of Chicago.

00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:22.356
He is a Core Faculty and Senior
Fellow at the Institute of

00:00:22.356 --> 00:00:27.227
Genomics and Systems Biology,
and the Computation Institute.

00:00:27.227 --> 00:00:31.031
He is also the founder and a
partner of Open Data Group,

00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:34.001
which has been building
predictive models over big data

00:00:34.001 --> 00:00:38.005
for Fortune 500
companies since 2002.

00:00:38.005 --> 00:00:42.142
His topic and title of his
presentation today is Big Data &amp;

00:00:42.142 --> 00:00:45.646
Analytics: Five Trends
and Five Challenges.

00:00:45.646 --> 00:00:48.448
Please join me in
welcoming, Dr. Grossman.

00:00:49.416 --> 00:00:53.487
(Applause)

00:01:38.632 --> 00:01:41.001
It's a real pleasure to be here.

00:01:42.703 --> 00:01:46.940
I'm going to give a high
level talk about Big Data.

00:01:48.709 --> 00:01:53.714
I'm going to assume you know
nothing about it, but assume you

00:01:53.714 --> 00:01:55.682
care a little bit about it.

00:01:57.684 --> 00:02:00.287
The term Big Data,
like Cloud computing,

00:02:00.287 --> 00:02:02.255
doesn't mean anything anymore.

00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:05.692
The simple way to tell when the
term doesn't mean anything is if

00:02:05.692 --> 00:02:09.963
you're in an airport and waiting
for your luggage and you see a

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:13.834
term like Cloud computing or
Big Data then, from a research

00:02:13.834 --> 00:02:17.771
perspective, you see the terms
in a large advertisement that's

00:02:17.771 --> 00:02:22.642
10 feet by 20 feet, then that's
really a good sign that that

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:26.046
term doesn't mean anything
from a research perspective.

00:02:26.046 --> 00:02:28.315
And so, Big Data is like that.

00:02:28.315 --> 00:02:34.688
This is a 2012 report: Big Data,
Big Impact from the World

00:02:34.688 --> 00:02:38.291
Economic Forum where they're
talking about how big data is

00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:42.896
going to impact the developing
world so it's a nice term to use

00:02:42.896 --> 00:02:45.665
but I'm going to try to dive
down a little bit into it.

00:02:45.665 --> 00:02:54.408
So... the other way to realize
that there's a certain

00:02:54.408 --> 00:02:57.344
trajectory to term is when
the term appears on the cover

00:02:57.344 --> 00:03:01.314
of magazines like Science,
Nature, The Economist, and

00:03:01.314 --> 00:03:03.917
Scientific America
and here are some covers.

00:03:03.917 --> 00:03:08.989
The traditional way of looking
at big data is to measure it in

00:03:08.989 --> 00:03:15.929
terms of bytes like megabytes
or petabytes and if you go to a

00:03:15.929 --> 00:03:20.834
conference in data and people
have a couple beers you hear

00:03:20.834 --> 00:03:22.903
them complaining that
when they reach a certain

00:03:22.903 --> 00:03:25.739
level of petabytes their
file systems stop working,

00:03:25.739 --> 00:03:28.975
their interconnects stop working
and so on, so there's clearly

00:03:28.975 --> 00:03:32.179
an issue, as you get more bytes,
that certain things

00:03:32.179 --> 00:03:33.080
begin to break.

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:38.418
The other way, because when
people popularize subjects,

00:03:38.418 --> 00:03:42.722
they like alliteration, is to
talk about data in terms of how

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:48.495
much there is, how fast it
moves, what the variety is, etc.

00:03:48.495 --> 00:03:50.931
And so, there's nothing wrong
with either of these methods but

00:03:50.931 --> 00:03:53.900
they miss a little bit about
what's associated and has

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.870
transitioned in big data,
and so, I want to dive a

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:58.772
little bit further.

00:03:58.772 --> 00:04:03.410
The simplest way to think
of data is that we always have

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:05.178
data, we always will.

00:04:05.178 --> 00:04:07.814
We'll always have people who can
do something with it and we'll

00:04:07.814 --> 00:04:11.284
always have people who throw
up their hands and complain.

00:04:11.284 --> 00:04:17.891
What does change is every
few years the terms change.

00:04:17.891 --> 00:04:24.731
I've been in the business
for about 35 years and I see,

00:04:24.731 --> 00:04:26.800
more or less, the same problems.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:32.239
I do notice there's an ebb and
flow to the meetings I go to so

00:04:32.239 --> 00:04:39.780
around '96 to 2001 there was
sort of a real interest in large

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.682
data and that was associated
around the term "data mining

00:04:42.682 --> 00:04:44.384
and knowledge discovery."

00:04:44.384 --> 00:04:47.187
And, starting a few years
ago, maybe 5 years ago,

00:04:47.187 --> 00:04:51.024
there was sort of a resurgence
of interest but, more or less,

00:04:51.024 --> 00:04:53.894
there's been a continuous
trajectory as we've

00:04:53.894 --> 00:04:54.628
changed the terms.

00:04:54.628 --> 00:04:59.099
What you see on the Google
Trends is the blue is the term

00:04:59.099 --> 00:05:02.536
"Data Mining" or "Knowledge
Discovery" and the red is the

00:05:02.536 --> 00:05:03.837
term "Big Data."

00:05:03.837 --> 00:05:07.207
So, if you look at the sum of
those two terms, nothing has

00:05:07.207 --> 00:05:10.577
changed, but we use the
terms slightly differently.

00:05:10.577 --> 00:05:14.881
I do think certain things have
changed, but it really is not

00:05:14.881 --> 00:05:17.617
whether you call it data mining,
statistics, or knowledge

00:05:17.617 --> 00:05:20.821
discovery, or
predictive analytics.

00:05:25.091 --> 00:05:28.128
Chaitan and I were just talking
and if you want a different

00:05:28.128 --> 00:05:31.331
perspective on data go to
a conference called Strata.

00:05:31.331 --> 00:05:36.236
You can't believe anything
you hear at that conference,

00:05:36.236 --> 00:05:39.506
unlike other conferences
where everything they say is

00:05:39.506 --> 00:05:42.976
absolutely true with no
exaggeration, but it's still

00:05:42.976 --> 00:05:46.279
important to go to a conference
like that because it fills sort

00:05:46.279 --> 00:05:50.417
of a large exhibit floor the
size of a football field and

00:05:50.417 --> 00:05:51.418
they're all doing things.

00:05:51.418 --> 00:05:56.223
And one of the things different
there is many of them come from

00:05:56.223 --> 00:05:59.326
companies where they have a lot
of data and they get fired if

00:05:59.326 --> 00:06:02.229
they do stupid things with it,
so, there's a certain Darwinian

00:06:02.229 --> 00:06:07.667
evolution that takes place and
the density of people who know

00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:10.370
what's going on is maybe not
that great, but there are a lot

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:12.706
of them there that know a lot.

00:06:12.706 --> 00:06:16.576
This definition is not a
bad working definition for

00:06:16.576 --> 00:06:17.978
big data today.

00:06:17.978 --> 00:06:21.681
Data is big today if it's too
big for your IT group to help

00:06:21.681 --> 00:06:25.919
you work with it, and if you
need something else, then the

00:06:25.919 --> 00:06:27.320
data is big.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:30.023
So, you know, depending upon
whether you're Google or whether

00:06:30.023 --> 00:06:35.895
you're a social science lab that
doesn't even get a data closet,

00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:42.002
the size will change but the
problem remains that you have a

00:06:42.002 --> 00:06:44.504
problem with managing your data.

00:06:45.472 --> 00:06:49.209
I think one of the real things
that does change, and this is

00:06:49.209 --> 00:06:54.614
because of the same law that
drives commoditization of

00:06:54.614 --> 00:07:00.654
processors, you know, Moore's
Law, that gives us every 18-24

00:07:00.654 --> 00:07:03.356
months, you know, that may
change in a few years, but right

00:07:03.356 --> 00:07:06.926
now we have a tremendous
increase in our processing power

00:07:06.926 --> 00:07:11.431
and so, we can do a level of
compute in every two years that

00:07:11.431 --> 00:07:16.269
is just sort of unmanageable
several years before that.

00:07:16.269 --> 00:07:19.673
That same commoditization
impacts sensors, and so,

00:07:19.673 --> 00:07:25.111
you have sensors putting
out more and more data at lower

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:26.379
and lower cost.

00:07:26.379 --> 00:07:29.649
And then, scientific
communities, as we're going to

00:07:29.649 --> 00:07:33.987
discuss, organize their sensors
differently, but in each case,

00:07:33.987 --> 00:07:36.589
the scientific communities
organize their sensors and put

00:07:36.589 --> 00:07:41.528
out data and that's impacting
the data we see in research.

00:07:41.528 --> 00:07:45.799
I think, perhaps, the most
fundamental transformation is

00:07:45.799 --> 00:07:50.603
every decade or so, the way we
use these sensors changes and we

00:07:50.603 --> 00:07:53.807
get incredibly new
modalities of data.

00:07:53.807 --> 00:07:59.179
So, when I started doing
commercial, large big data,

00:07:59.179 --> 00:08:04.417
in the mid-90's, there was
Internet click data and Points

00:08:04.417 --> 00:08:08.555
of Transaction data and no
one knew how to analyze it.

00:08:08.555 --> 00:08:13.526
People basically created what
now are called Beowulf clusters.

00:08:13.526 --> 00:08:16.162
They created clusters of
computers, they created new

00:08:16.162 --> 00:08:20.066
statistical techniques like
ensembles of models which fit

00:08:20.066 --> 00:08:23.937
very well over clusters of
computers and over time they

00:08:23.937 --> 00:08:27.006
were able to analyze
data at that scale.

00:08:27.006 --> 00:08:30.877
Around 2000, a whole 'nother
type of data came out which is

00:08:30.877 --> 00:08:35.382
the unstructured text data
on the web, and then, again,

00:08:35.382 --> 00:08:42.989
what emerged was sort of the
map-reduced style that Google

00:08:42.989 --> 00:08:46.693
introduced to analyze large
unstructured data that comes

00:08:46.693 --> 00:08:47.727
up in search.

00:08:47.727 --> 00:08:50.096
And that was actually
a fundamental change.

00:08:50.096 --> 00:08:53.099
And then what's going on
now is there's yet another

00:08:53.099 --> 00:08:57.771
transformation in which things
are moving from the Internet to

00:08:57.771 --> 00:09:00.573
mobile and Internet of
Things and that's creating

00:09:00.573 --> 00:09:01.941
a third revolution.

00:09:01.941 --> 00:09:05.879
Now, one of the underlying
themes here is none of these

00:09:05.879 --> 00:09:10.717
revolutions are from the
scientific side and in each of

00:09:10.717 --> 00:09:15.955
these cases, for whatever
reasons, not only did the 10 -

00:09:15.955 --> 00:09:21.027
or 100 - or 1000-fold increase
in data come from the industry

00:09:21.027 --> 00:09:25.598
side, but the new techniques, in
terms of hardware and software

00:09:25.598 --> 00:09:27.434
stacks came from there, too.

00:09:27.434 --> 00:09:32.172
And in each case, at least in my
experience, the research lags

00:09:32.172 --> 00:09:34.908
and then picks up these
techniques, sometimes a few

00:09:34.908 --> 00:09:37.310
years later, but more
often a decade later.

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:42.482
And so, the transition
where a number of years ago,

00:09:42.482 --> 00:09:45.685
high performance computing
and simulation would lead,

00:09:45.685 --> 00:09:50.089
you see in data science that
researchers, by and large,

00:09:50.089 --> 00:09:55.395
follow and that's for the simple
reason that if you are hired by

00:09:55.395 --> 00:10:00.366
a large internet or mobile
company today, you will have

00:10:00.366 --> 00:10:04.103
responsibility for working with
very large data sets with no

00:10:04.103 --> 00:10:09.909
nonsense ability to deliver out
working models, and that will be

00:10:09.909 --> 00:10:12.946
at least one or two orders
of magnitude larger than

00:10:12.946 --> 00:10:15.215
what you would get the
way we train students at

00:10:15.215 --> 00:10:16.616
most universities here.

00:10:16.616 --> 00:10:20.420
So, there's a big gap and unless
your students go and intern in

00:10:20.420 --> 00:10:23.056
one of these companies,
there's a big gap in how we

00:10:23.056 --> 00:10:25.792
think of data.

00:10:25.792 --> 00:10:31.130
Now, if you think of this from
a portfolio of discovery point

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:35.235
of view, just because data is
big doesn't mean the discoveries

00:10:35.235 --> 00:10:40.240
are important and that's just
important to keep in mind.

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:43.776
We talk a lot about big data,
on the other hand, in terms of,

00:10:43.776 --> 00:10:48.948
you know, we run a data
intensive computing facility

00:10:48.948 --> 00:10:54.387
with several hundred users,
several petabytes of data that

00:10:54.387 --> 00:10:58.157
is scientific research data
that's available for discovery

00:10:58.157 --> 00:11:01.294
and I'm very interested
each year in the quality of

00:11:01.294 --> 00:11:03.263
the science that comes.

00:11:03.263 --> 00:11:08.535
Each month we give away
computing hours, over a petabyte

00:11:08.535 --> 00:11:11.938
of data, that's about a million
dollars at Amazon rates for each

00:11:11.938 --> 00:11:13.840
of our facilities,
and we have several.

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:17.510
And then you can ask,
is if we do a portfolio of,

00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:22.415
we give this many, and so, I
think we have a couple million

00:11:22.415 --> 00:11:25.618
core hours per facility and the
question is, if we could give

00:11:25.618 --> 00:11:28.955
people, for data intensive
science, say 300,000 or half

00:11:28.955 --> 00:11:32.892
a million that month versus
10,000 as we do that in

00:11:32.892 --> 00:11:36.095
sort of a logarithmic
distribution in terms of core

00:11:36.095 --> 00:11:39.432
hours over a petabyte of data,
you don't always get the best

00:11:39.432 --> 00:11:42.435
science from the
largest allocation.

00:11:42.435 --> 00:11:45.071
And so, you have to sort
of think of how that works.

00:11:45.071 --> 00:11:49.475
And a simple example,
Netflix is small data.

00:11:49.475 --> 00:11:52.278
My definition of small
means if it's in memory.

00:11:52.278 --> 00:11:58.785
So, the Netflix prize, which was
a million dollar prize, was for

00:11:58.785 --> 00:12:06.826
7 years of data, over half a
million customers, a hundred

00:12:06.826 --> 00:12:09.996
million movie ratings, it was a
very tough statistical problem

00:12:09.996 --> 00:12:15.501
to solve, but it was small in
the sense it fit into memory,

00:12:15.501 --> 00:12:18.871
or it does basically fit
into a single work station,

00:12:18.871 --> 00:12:22.175
and they designed that so they
could get 50,000 people to give

00:12:22.175 --> 00:12:23.810
them free labor.

00:12:24.944 --> 00:12:27.914
I've already alluded to this,
but I want to talk just for a

00:12:27.914 --> 00:12:30.383
minute about the origins of
big data because I think it's

00:12:30.383 --> 00:12:33.953
important before we go
back to the scientific data.

00:12:38.825 --> 00:12:41.561
If you have data and you can't
manage it, there are three

00:12:41.561 --> 00:12:43.229
things you can do in general.

00:12:43.229 --> 00:12:46.566
You can change the hardware,
you can change the software,

00:12:46.566 --> 00:12:49.202
or you could change the
algorithm, and then sometimes

00:12:49.202 --> 00:12:50.703
you can change
the problem you ask.

00:12:50.703 --> 00:12:54.474
Let's just look at how
we change hardware.

00:12:54.474 --> 00:12:56.843
If we change hardware
there are two fundamental

00:12:56.843 --> 00:12:57.977
things we could do.

00:12:57.977 --> 00:13:00.046
We could do what's
called scale up.

00:13:00.046 --> 00:13:04.617
We get more specialized hardware
by adding specialized components

00:13:04.617 --> 00:13:07.754
or building specialized devices,
or we could do what's called

00:13:07.754 --> 00:13:11.190
scale out where we add
more of the devices.

00:13:11.190 --> 00:13:16.496
And when we do HBC, when we
do large data, we can take any

00:13:16.496 --> 00:13:19.165
combination in this
two-dimensional space,

00:13:19.165 --> 00:13:21.768
and the interesting thing is,
I mentioned there was a

00:13:21.768 --> 00:13:25.004
new source of data which is
large amounts of unstructured

00:13:25.004 --> 00:13:26.372
data from the web.

00:13:26.372 --> 00:13:31.244
And Google made a series
of interesting choices.

00:13:31.244 --> 00:13:36.482
A) They decided that they would
not follow the dominant paradigm

00:13:36.482 --> 00:13:40.486
at that time, which was to use
fairly specialized computes with

00:13:40.486 --> 00:13:44.290
a scale up and then use
a software infrastructure

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:45.658
called message passing.

00:13:45.658 --> 00:13:51.030
Instead, they created what is
now called map reduce and the

00:13:51.030 --> 00:13:54.834
Google file system and we
can use it in a hadoop and they

00:13:54.834 --> 00:13:58.638
scale it out over commodity
components with a new software

00:13:58.638 --> 00:14:02.542
infrastructure that's now part
of hadoop and sort of that

00:14:02.542 --> 00:14:05.011
large ecosystem you
would see at Strata.

00:14:05.011 --> 00:14:07.780
And that was driven by
computational advertising.

00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:10.950
And why did computational
advertising drive that?

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:14.420
It drove it for the simple
reason that if you made a small

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:17.690
improvement in computational
advertising, you generated an

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:19.325
additional billion dollars.

00:14:19.325 --> 00:14:23.396
And every time you had an
additional billion dollars,

00:14:23.396 --> 00:14:26.299
you could save it to improve the
hardware and software stack and

00:14:26.299 --> 00:14:28.034
that created a virtuous loop.

00:14:28.034 --> 00:14:31.537
And we're going to hear later
from the NSF, but my current

00:14:31.537 --> 00:14:34.440
understanding is, in data
intensive science, they spend

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:39.512
less than a billion dollars each
year, so that gave Google and

00:14:39.512 --> 00:14:43.182
some of these others a strong
competitive advantage that build

00:14:43.182 --> 00:14:47.019
year after year and
created this gap.

00:14:47.019 --> 00:14:51.157
So, it is now a 120
billion dollar industry,

00:14:51.157 --> 00:14:53.025
just online advertising.

00:14:53.025 --> 00:14:56.496
This is the inside of Google's
Data Center, and this is

00:14:56.496 --> 00:15:00.867
probably larger than the data
centers because it costs 400-500

00:15:00.867 --> 00:15:04.537
million dollars than the data
centers at many universities.

00:15:06.372 --> 00:15:10.510
The core software infrastructure
that came out was released in a

00:15:10.510 --> 00:15:13.780
series of papers that
conveniently came out when

00:15:13.780 --> 00:15:16.749
Google was working on
the next generation.

00:15:16.749 --> 00:15:22.955
It was a software staff with
a very scalable file system,

00:15:22.955 --> 00:15:26.959
a very scalable way to work with
unstructured text data over it,

00:15:26.959 --> 00:15:31.164
and then a very
scalable database.

00:15:31.164 --> 00:15:35.034
And this is a picture from a
couple years ago of the 120

00:15:35.034 --> 00:15:39.572
billion dollar industry that is
around computational advertising

00:15:39.572 --> 00:15:42.175
and created these
new techniques.

00:15:43.776 --> 00:15:47.747
And this led to a new way of
talking about the size of data.

00:15:47.747 --> 00:15:51.083
You talked about the size of
data in terms of the number of

00:15:51.083 --> 00:15:54.420
megawatts of the data center
that controlled the data,

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:59.125
so, Facebook would say they're
building a new 30 megawatt data

00:15:59.125 --> 00:16:02.895
center for, say, 300 million
dollars, and that would be to

00:16:02.895 --> 00:16:05.298
not only hold the data,
but to analyze it and to

00:16:05.298 --> 00:16:06.432
make it actionable.

00:16:06.432 --> 00:16:10.269
So, that's sort of the unit, you
know, say 10 to 15 megawatts,

00:16:10.269 --> 00:16:15.141
is the unit that they think of
of data, and one of the themes,

00:16:15.141 --> 00:16:19.078
I think, that's interesting from
a research perspective is what

00:16:19.078 --> 00:16:22.515
is the unit that we should
think of data from a research

00:16:22.515 --> 00:16:27.253
perspective so that we can make
advances that are unimaginable

00:16:27.253 --> 00:16:30.056
if we don't conceptualize
the problem right.

00:16:30.056 --> 00:16:32.792
So, what is the
analogy of this unit?

00:16:35.027 --> 00:16:37.930
I want to talk a little
bit about analytics.

00:16:37.930 --> 00:16:41.200
That's some self tracking
data from someone walking

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:42.635
around San Francisco.

00:16:48.774 --> 00:16:51.711
The reason I'm going to start
with analytics and then go to

00:16:51.711 --> 00:16:54.447
statistical models, you know,
in research, what we need to do

00:16:54.447 --> 00:16:57.650
is to take data, integrate it,
bring it together,

00:16:57.650 --> 00:17:00.753
build statistical models, build
distributive statistical models,

00:17:00.753 --> 00:17:02.021
and make discoveries.

00:17:02.021 --> 00:17:06.259
So, we go from data
to statistical models to

00:17:06.259 --> 00:17:09.028
discoveries to validation
and we have to do that in a

00:17:09.028 --> 00:17:10.863
very scalable way.

00:17:10.863 --> 00:17:15.067
The driving focus in
industry is analytics.

00:17:15.067 --> 00:17:23.109
It's using, in the jargon of the
Fair Credit Reporting Act, FCRA,

00:17:23.109 --> 00:17:28.447
is to build statistical models
that are statistically valid

00:17:28.447 --> 00:17:30.616
and empirically derived.

00:17:32.318 --> 00:17:37.757
I pulled from this ASA,
the American Statistical

00:17:37.757 --> 00:17:43.029
Association website, their
current definition on their

00:17:43.029 --> 00:17:45.631
website of statistics.

00:17:45.631 --> 00:17:48.935
It's a much more complicated
story than this, but it's really

00:17:48.935 --> 00:17:52.071
focused around uncertainty.

00:17:52.071 --> 00:17:56.309
Some of the best advances in big
data come from statisticians,

00:17:56.309 --> 00:18:00.713
but, there's a wide variety
within the statistical community

00:18:00.713 --> 00:18:03.749
of, is big data real?

00:18:03.749 --> 00:18:06.452
I mean, in a certain sense,
statisticians are right when

00:18:06.452 --> 00:18:09.055
they say there is no
such thing as big data.

00:18:09.055 --> 00:18:12.558
They're either empirically
driven and statistically valid

00:18:12.558 --> 00:18:13.926
models or not.

00:18:13.926 --> 00:18:18.097
On the other hand, as you get
new sources of data that are 100

00:18:18.097 --> 00:18:22.802
or 1,000 larger than before, the
types of statistical models you

00:18:22.802 --> 00:18:26.339
build tend to be different and
it's sort of that tension that's

00:18:26.339 --> 00:18:31.644
one of the interesting focal
points of the field right now.

00:18:31.644 --> 00:18:37.249
This is a very busy slide,
but occasionally, well once,

00:18:37.249 --> 00:18:41.087
on an airplane, someone asked
me what I did and so, I tried to

00:18:41.087 --> 00:18:45.891
explain what's happened in big
data in the last 40 years and

00:18:45.891 --> 00:18:47.593
it's all in this slide.

00:18:47.593 --> 00:18:51.364
My view these days is there's
very few changes, but once a

00:18:51.364 --> 00:18:53.299
decade we get a change.

00:18:53.299 --> 00:18:57.937
If you go to Strata and read the
slogans of the marketing people,

00:18:57.937 --> 00:19:00.840
you feel that every 10 minutes
there's a change and it came

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:03.542
from that venture backed company
that's going to be gone in

00:19:03.542 --> 00:19:07.179
10 minutes whose technology will
forever disappear, but from a

00:19:07.179 --> 00:19:09.382
broader perspective,
there really hasn't been

00:19:09.382 --> 00:19:10.549
that many changes.

00:19:10.549 --> 00:19:13.285
So, the first change is
what we call the field.

00:19:13.285 --> 00:19:14.820
That's now fundamental.

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:19.091
The second change is what kind
of conferences do you go to and

00:19:19.091 --> 00:19:20.960
how nice are they?

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:24.897
When data mining came up the
conferences became much nicer in

00:19:24.897 --> 00:19:28.634
much nicer places with much
nicer hotels than when it was

00:19:28.634 --> 00:19:31.337
called computationally
intensive statistics.

00:19:31.337 --> 00:19:35.708
And recently, with big data,
the conferences are even nicer.

00:19:35.708 --> 00:19:39.011
So, I think from that viewpoint
there's been a real advance in

00:19:39.011 --> 00:19:42.348
the field because the
conferences are much nicer.

00:19:44.050 --> 00:19:50.456
If you read the proceedings of
one of these conferences every

00:19:50.456 --> 00:19:54.060
year we get another 200
algorithms that are "from the

00:19:54.060 --> 00:19:56.629
abstract, innovative and
are going to fundamentally

00:19:56.629 --> 00:19:57.830
change the field."

00:19:57.830 --> 00:20:00.166
On the other hand,
if you look back,

00:20:00.166 --> 00:20:02.501
the number of algorithms
we get that are really

00:20:02.501 --> 00:20:05.971
impactful, we seem to
get one a decade or so.

00:20:05.971 --> 00:20:08.908
Sometimes we'll get a new
algorithm, we'll take an old

00:20:08.908 --> 00:20:12.411
algorithm over larger compute
and change this name, say,

00:20:12.411 --> 00:20:15.281
from NeuroNet to Deep Learning,
but we're still getting a new

00:20:15.281 --> 00:20:17.249
algorithm every decade or so.

00:20:17.249 --> 00:20:22.755
We had classification tree,
we have NeuroNets, then we had

00:20:22.755 --> 00:20:25.958
classification regression trees,
we'd support vector machines,

00:20:25.958 --> 00:20:29.328
we had a graph algorithm in the
page rank and now I don't know

00:20:29.328 --> 00:20:31.430
quite what the
best algorithm is.

00:20:31.430 --> 00:20:33.599
Every time I go to one
of these conferences I try

00:20:33.599 --> 00:20:34.700
to take a vote.

00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:36.302
Some people say
it's belief metrics.

00:20:36.302 --> 00:20:39.872
Maybe it's the globally
distributed algorithms, but I'm

00:20:39.872 --> 00:20:42.441
sure we have an algorithm and
we're going to figure out what

00:20:42.441 --> 00:20:47.446
it is and we'll look back and
it'll be obvious, but we have

00:20:47.446 --> 00:20:49.982
a lot of graph algorithms.

00:20:51.183 --> 00:20:53.919
Pretty fundamental is, I talked
about the hardware set,

00:20:53.919 --> 00:20:55.521
the software changes.

00:20:55.521 --> 00:20:57.490
So, I talked about hadoop.

00:20:57.490 --> 00:21:01.927
Hadoop really is useful and
it's being used in computing.

00:21:01.927 --> 00:21:03.162
There's some issues with it.

00:21:03.162 --> 00:21:06.866
We got R, we got PostgreSQL,
and then the scale which

00:21:06.866 --> 00:21:08.334
we compute changes.

00:21:08.334 --> 00:21:11.537
We went from a computer to
a Beowulf cluster; that is,

00:21:11.537 --> 00:21:17.309
what could fit in a rack, to
a data center and I'm going to

00:21:17.309 --> 00:21:20.479
come back to that because
one of the questions is what is

00:21:20.479 --> 00:21:23.516
the analogy of a data
center for data intensive

00:21:23.516 --> 00:21:25.251
computing for research.

00:21:25.251 --> 00:21:31.624
And then, around 2011, Google
released algorithms that scale

00:21:31.624 --> 00:21:33.592
across multiple data centers.

00:21:36.228 --> 00:21:40.900
This is a very old problem, but
I've talked a lot about things,

00:21:40.900 --> 00:21:44.003
but a lot of these
ideas are very, very old.

00:21:44.003 --> 00:21:49.308
This is a problem that Abraham
Wald worked on in World War II.

00:21:49.308 --> 00:21:54.647
The question was, how do you put
additional armor on airplanes?

00:21:54.647 --> 00:21:57.683
I think most people have heard
this, but it's worth thinking

00:21:57.683 --> 00:21:58.684
back about this.

00:21:58.684 --> 00:22:04.723
Airplanes were being shot down
in World War II and if you armor

00:22:04.723 --> 00:22:08.460
the whole plane it can't take
off, if you armor the wrong

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:12.264
places, it doesn't help, and
if you armor the right places

00:22:12.264 --> 00:22:14.466
you save lives.

00:22:14.466 --> 00:22:19.138
So, when they collect the data,
you can see when a plane comes

00:22:19.138 --> 00:22:23.809
back where the bullet holes are,
so the question is, how do you

00:22:23.809 --> 00:22:26.745
build a statistical model from
knowing where the bullet holes

00:22:26.745 --> 00:22:30.049
are in an airplane that will
harden the airplane and allow

00:22:30.049 --> 00:22:32.084
more airplanes to come back?

00:22:32.084 --> 00:22:34.420
And notice the validation
is quite simple.

00:22:34.420 --> 00:22:38.190
If you put a constant number of
airplanes in the field and you

00:22:38.190 --> 00:22:42.094
assume that the adversary
is doing constant behavior,

00:22:42.094 --> 00:22:44.797
then your algorithm works if
the number of airplanes that

00:22:44.797 --> 00:22:46.131
comes back increases.

00:22:46.131 --> 00:22:50.736
If you think about this, and
Abraham Wald did a model,

00:22:50.736 --> 00:22:55.741
where you want to put the
armor is where the bullet holes

00:22:55.741 --> 00:22:58.644
aren't because those are
the planes that didn't get

00:22:58.644 --> 00:23:01.280
back and he wrote
a paper about that.

00:23:01.280 --> 00:23:05.684
That is a nice example of what's
called selection bias and it's a

00:23:05.684 --> 00:23:09.455
nice example that you still need
to think, no matter what the

00:23:09.455 --> 00:23:13.092
software or hardware is, you
still really need to think about

00:23:13.092 --> 00:23:15.094
the statistical and algorithms.

00:23:15.094 --> 00:23:20.699
And so, that notion is still
very, very valuable today.

00:23:20.699 --> 00:23:25.437
One of the themes here is the
bigger the data is the easier it

00:23:25.437 --> 00:23:27.006
is to be stupid.

00:23:29.308 --> 00:23:33.312
I want to stop about the
historical origins of big data.

00:23:33.312 --> 00:23:39.952
The first emergence in terms
of data mining came with the

00:23:39.952 --> 00:23:43.689
emergence of the Internet
and the data that came

00:23:43.689 --> 00:23:44.657
from click streams.

00:23:44.657 --> 00:23:51.764
The second renaissance with big
data came when people analyzed

00:23:51.764 --> 00:23:55.067
data at the scale of a data
center from what Google's work

00:23:55.067 --> 00:23:58.904
was, and talk about how
this looks like in Science.

00:23:58.904 --> 00:24:02.641
And most of the time, when you
talk to people about big data,

00:24:02.641 --> 00:24:04.276
they give you one example.

00:24:04.276 --> 00:24:09.415
This is the example when a group
of researchers worked together

00:24:09.415 --> 00:24:11.383
to come up with one theme.

00:24:11.383 --> 00:24:13.185
It cost a billion dollars.

00:24:13.185 --> 00:24:16.655
They get it funded, maybe 10
billion dollars, and they build

00:24:16.655 --> 00:24:20.993
one instrument consisting of
lots and lots of small sensors

00:24:20.993 --> 00:24:23.295
and they produce data.

00:24:23.295 --> 00:24:26.899
Usually this takes 10 years and,
at the beginning of the 10

00:24:26.899 --> 00:24:30.336
years, the amount of data that's
being produced looks scary,

00:24:30.336 --> 00:24:32.504
but by the end of the 10
years when the instrument's

00:24:32.504 --> 00:24:35.774
actually built, it turns out
to be a fairly modest amount

00:24:35.774 --> 00:24:37.343
of data, if you know
what you're doing.

00:24:37.343 --> 00:24:42.014
For example, the LHC, the Large
Hadron Collider, produces -

00:24:42.014 --> 00:24:43.515
raw data is about 15 petabytes.

00:24:43.515 --> 00:24:47.353
It's multiplied significantly as
it's analyzed, but the raw data

00:24:47.353 --> 00:24:51.090
is 15 petabytes, which is not
a big number today, even though

00:24:51.090 --> 00:24:52.991
the dry data is
quite a bit bigger.

00:24:52.991 --> 00:24:59.131
Another modality for researchers
is when you have say hundreds to

00:24:59.131 --> 00:25:05.104
thousands of labs producing,
again, instruments that produce

00:25:05.104 --> 00:25:11.877
lots of data, but it comes on
these sort of midsized machines

00:25:11.877 --> 00:25:14.913
that a lab might spend
a million dollars on.

00:25:14.913 --> 00:25:18.550
From a couple years ago, this is
the sequencing machine that sort

00:25:18.550 --> 00:25:23.722
of changed how NextGen
sequencing was done.

00:25:23.722 --> 00:25:28.227
This is produced in aggregate,
a lot of data across biologies.

00:25:28.227 --> 00:25:33.499
It created a crisis that, after
only 5 to 8 years of discussion

00:25:33.499 --> 00:25:38.804
the biological community
created sort of a C change in

00:25:38.804 --> 00:25:39.772
how they do things.

00:25:39.772 --> 00:25:44.143
I'm going to talk a little
bit about that in my talk.

00:25:44.143 --> 00:25:46.879
This is one of the
areas I work in.

00:25:46.879 --> 00:25:51.884
This happened a
number of years ago.

00:25:51.884 --> 00:25:54.820
We discussed it for a number of
years and we're beginning to

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:58.190
change the way we look at large
amounts of genomics data.

00:25:58.190 --> 00:26:02.961
Something that's just happening
now is... raise your hand if you

00:26:02.961 --> 00:26:05.230
have any self tracking device?

00:26:05.898 --> 00:26:06.265
Okay.

00:26:06.265 --> 00:26:09.968
Raise your hand if you know,
in addition to yourself -

00:26:09.968 --> 00:26:12.271
this is unrelated to the talk,
I'm just sort of curious -

00:26:12.271 --> 00:26:18.010
if, in addition to yourself,
what third party companies make

00:26:18.010 --> 00:26:19.578
use of your self tracking?

00:26:19.578 --> 00:26:23.482
Does anyone track carefully
who else has their

00:26:23.482 --> 00:26:24.383
self tracking data?

00:26:24.383 --> 00:26:25.717
Raise your hand.

00:26:25.717 --> 00:26:26.885
Okay.

00:26:26.885 --> 00:26:29.188
That's a whole 'nother talk,
but I'm not going to

00:26:29.188 --> 00:26:30.189
talk about that.

00:26:30.189 --> 00:26:31.723
But, this is changing things.

00:26:31.723 --> 00:26:33.826
I'm going to come back to this.

00:26:33.826 --> 00:26:39.898
One of the ways that people
think about this and, you know,

00:26:39.898 --> 00:26:44.503
I think the MORE Foundation, the
Sloan Foundation, really took

00:26:44.503 --> 00:26:48.841
a step in sort of trying to get
research universities to think

00:26:48.841 --> 00:26:54.246
fundamentally about how to
do interdisciplinary science.

00:26:54.246 --> 00:26:58.350
Now, the term Data Science was
introduced in industry a number

00:26:58.350 --> 00:27:03.222
of years ago and it's also been
used for something pretty

00:27:03.222 --> 00:27:07.359
different in academic research
for an emerging discipline.

00:27:07.359 --> 00:27:10.729
And, again, this is sort of the
MORE and Sloan Foundations

00:27:10.729 --> 00:27:15.701
agreed to work together in an
initiative around Data Science,

00:27:15.701 --> 00:27:18.804
and so, this is the
term I'm going to use.

00:27:18.804 --> 00:27:23.609
You could think of Data Science
as what happens when I take deep

00:27:23.609 --> 00:27:27.646
math and stat, when I take deep
computer science and I take deep

00:27:27.646 --> 00:27:30.182
knowledge of a field and
I bring them together.

00:27:30.182 --> 00:27:33.118
The MORE Foundation and the
Sloan Foundation talk about

00:27:33.118 --> 00:27:35.687
pie-shaped science.

00:27:35.687 --> 00:27:38.090
Pie-shaped science
means you have two legs.

00:27:38.090 --> 00:27:40.692
You have a leg into the
discipline where you have a

00:27:40.692 --> 00:27:43.495
deep knowledge of the
discipline... biology, social

00:27:43.495 --> 00:27:46.832
science, humanities... and you
have a leg into math, stat,

00:27:46.832 --> 00:27:47.966
and computer science.

00:27:47.966 --> 00:27:50.903
I split them, they don't;
and that you could bring those

00:27:50.903 --> 00:27:52.871
things together to
create a new science.

00:27:52.871 --> 00:27:58.076
And this is like any number
of sciences, starting with

00:27:58.076 --> 00:28:02.247
statistics to computer science,
from an academic discipline

00:28:02.247 --> 00:28:05.684
point of view, it's still up
in the air whether this is a

00:28:05.684 --> 00:28:08.487
separate discipline or whether
there's a data science for

00:28:08.487 --> 00:28:11.023
physics, a data science
for biology, a data science

00:28:11.023 --> 00:28:12.090
for social science.

00:28:12.090 --> 00:28:15.394
I think in some sense it
doesn't matter, but there are

00:28:15.394 --> 00:28:19.298
interesting challenges that are
new in data science horizontal

00:28:19.298 --> 00:28:21.333
or data science vertical.

00:28:22.901 --> 00:28:27.272
In addition to showing you nice
things that happen, I also want

00:28:27.272 --> 00:28:29.241
to give you a few
cautionary tales.

00:28:29.241 --> 00:28:33.445
How many had salmon
in the last week?

00:28:33.445 --> 00:28:37.215
Okay, you may not want
to listen to the next...

00:28:37.215 --> 00:28:39.484
so, again, this is
pretty famous.

00:28:39.484 --> 00:28:42.487
That's a picture of a salmon,
of the type we're going

00:28:42.487 --> 00:28:44.156
to talk about.

00:28:46.191 --> 00:28:50.596
And this is a poster
at a meeting.

00:28:50.596 --> 00:28:52.898
I'll give you a
little more history.

00:28:52.898 --> 00:28:58.136
There's a lot of research now
where they put people into FMRI

00:28:58.136 --> 00:29:03.108
machines, show them pictures,
or tell them stories, or put

00:29:03.108 --> 00:29:07.446
chemicals into them and see what
parts of the brain light up.

00:29:10.015 --> 00:29:12.384
The experiment
was the following.

00:29:12.384 --> 00:29:14.186
That's the salmon.

00:29:14.186 --> 00:29:18.457
They took a dead salmon and
they put the dead salmon into a

00:29:18.457 --> 00:29:22.260
functional MRI machine and
they applied exactly the same

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:26.765
algorithms that were used
in about 20% of the published

00:29:26.765 --> 00:29:31.036
literature of these functional
MRI and emotional states and the

00:29:31.036 --> 00:29:32.471
voxels that light up.

00:29:32.471 --> 00:29:36.908
And they showed the dead salmon
pictures of happy humans and

00:29:36.908 --> 00:29:41.380
pictures of unhappy humans and
it was a controlled experiment.

00:29:41.380 --> 00:29:44.850
And then they identified
the voxel in the brain that

00:29:44.850 --> 00:29:49.388
identified happy humans because
that's presumably important in

00:29:49.388 --> 00:29:53.892
understanding the physiology
and the psychology of salmon.

00:29:53.892 --> 00:29:58.397
And, for some reason, the
journal would not accept this

00:29:58.397 --> 00:30:02.567
for publication, but they did
accept it as a poster paper

00:30:02.567 --> 00:30:10.942
and, you know, the references on
here, underneath, on my slides,

00:30:10.942 --> 00:30:14.346
you could see who did it,
but this is a classic problem.

00:30:14.346 --> 00:30:15.213
It's a classic problem.

00:30:15.213 --> 00:30:17.949
As you get more data,
it's easy to ignore

00:30:17.949 --> 00:30:19.184
multiple independent tests.

00:30:19.184 --> 00:30:23.789
There's standard techniques to
normalize out significant levels

00:30:23.789 --> 00:30:25.557
when you have multiple
independent tests.

00:30:25.557 --> 00:30:28.527
They were treating each of the
voxels as independent and asking

00:30:28.527 --> 00:30:32.664
if it rose above the noise level
and then, without correcting for

00:30:32.664 --> 00:30:36.568
the number of voxels, which is
thousands - tens of thousands.

00:30:36.568 --> 00:30:38.570
And this problem
happens all the time.

00:30:38.570 --> 00:30:41.540
It's a well known problem,
but that doesn't mean that the

00:30:41.540 --> 00:30:47.746
papers that are published
are aware of the problem.

00:30:47.746 --> 00:30:50.415
I've said this several times,
I just thought I'd remind you.

00:30:50.415 --> 00:30:54.252
The bigger the data, the easier
it is to do something stupid.

00:30:56.121 --> 00:30:56.722
Ah!

00:30:56.722 --> 00:30:58.457
There were only 8,000 voxels.

00:30:58.457 --> 00:31:03.128
A lot of scientists are doing
things and we have millions and

00:31:03.128 --> 00:31:05.664
billions of models, so you
really have to think through

00:31:05.664 --> 00:31:09.067
that you don't want to make
this simple voxel mistake.

00:31:10.569 --> 00:31:19.111
So, I want to leave you with one
or two thoughts to think about.

00:31:19.111 --> 00:31:24.649
The first thought is new
science, you know, I think a lot

00:31:24.649 --> 00:31:27.719
about how to create, you know,
I'm the director of a center for

00:31:27.719 --> 00:31:30.322
data intensive science at
the University of Chicago.

00:31:30.322 --> 00:31:35.460
There are several other centers
around data at the University

00:31:35.460 --> 00:31:40.799
and our center and the others
are thinking together and what

00:31:40.799 --> 00:31:44.069
is the academic discipline
around data science.

00:31:44.069 --> 00:31:48.673
And so, my point of view on this
is pretty simple, is that a lot

00:31:48.673 --> 00:31:54.679
of times a new discipline
or a new modality of science

00:31:54.679 --> 00:31:56.648
originates in an instrument.

00:31:56.648 --> 00:32:00.752
So, in the same way microscopes
gave us an instrument to look at

00:32:00.752 --> 00:32:04.189
small things and telescopes gave
us an instrument to look at far

00:32:04.189 --> 00:32:07.025
things, we could ask what
is the instrument we need to

00:32:07.025 --> 00:32:09.060
look at large data?

00:32:10.729 --> 00:32:15.801
And it's kind of interesting to
see what the resolution increase

00:32:15.801 --> 00:32:19.504
was in some of these instruments
that created new science.

00:32:19.504 --> 00:32:25.510
So, for example, the telescope
gave you about a 30x increase in

00:32:25.510 --> 00:32:27.512
resolution of the naked eye.

00:32:27.512 --> 00:32:33.018
The microscope gave you about
a 250 increase in resolution

00:32:33.018 --> 00:32:35.053
over the naked eye.

00:32:36.188 --> 00:32:39.491
When I was starting my careers,
people were distinguishing the

00:32:39.491 --> 00:32:42.761
theoretical and experimental
sciences for what they call

00:32:42.761 --> 00:32:46.331
simulation science where you
would take a computer, here a

00:32:46.331 --> 00:32:49.835
Cray computer, and do things
that couldn't be done before.

00:32:49.835 --> 00:32:54.840
As best as I can tell from
looking at history, that first

00:32:54.840 --> 00:32:58.710
Cray-1, which was a great
supercomputer and elegant in

00:32:58.710 --> 00:33:04.482
design, gave you a 10 to 100x
increase in resolution in speed

00:33:04.482 --> 00:33:07.853
over the high end
mainframes at that time.

00:33:09.654 --> 00:33:14.893
If you do a back of the envelope
computation, the data centers

00:33:14.893 --> 00:33:20.065
being built by industry give you
at least 100 fold increase in

00:33:20.065 --> 00:33:23.835
resolution, the ability to do
statistically valid models over

00:33:23.835 --> 00:33:26.771
data than what we have
in today's academic,

00:33:26.771 --> 00:33:28.673
most academic institutions.

00:33:28.673 --> 00:33:32.978
So, there are sort of
only two conclusions.

00:33:32.978 --> 00:33:37.949
One, you could say, because of
all the things we're exposed to

00:33:37.949 --> 00:33:41.620
in our diets these days, that
we're just not as smart as we

00:33:41.620 --> 00:33:45.290
were in these other ages and
that even though we have an

00:33:45.290 --> 00:33:49.661
instrument with maybe 10x
more resolution than the prior

00:33:49.661 --> 00:33:52.197
instruments we're not going
to create a new science;

00:33:52.197 --> 00:33:55.267
or, the other one is there's a
new science and we're going to

00:33:55.267 --> 00:33:57.402
eventually figure it out.

00:33:57.402 --> 00:33:59.905
It may not be interesting, it
may be interesting, but there'll

00:33:59.905 --> 00:34:01.373
be something there.

00:34:01.373 --> 00:34:05.710
I think a lot about how do
we build these instruments for

00:34:05.710 --> 00:34:10.682
ourselves and this is sort of my
bet for what the instrument's

00:34:10.682 --> 00:34:14.519
going to look like and where
I've spent the last 10 years

00:34:14.519 --> 00:34:16.488
or so, thinking about.

00:34:16.488 --> 00:34:20.625
We don't have a name and so
because someone here made the

00:34:20.625 --> 00:34:23.929
mistake of inviting me to
give this talk I'm going to

00:34:23.929 --> 00:34:25.297
call it a Cyberpod.

00:34:25.297 --> 00:34:29.234
This is mainly to irritate
everyone in the audience so

00:34:29.234 --> 00:34:33.505
you'll come up after the talk
and tell me that's the stupidest

00:34:33.505 --> 00:34:37.976
name that I've ever heard and
why don't you call it, you know,

00:34:37.976 --> 00:34:40.011
a tomato or something like that.

00:34:40.011 --> 00:34:42.414
So, I'm going to
call it a Cyberpod.

00:34:42.414 --> 00:34:44.883
It's something you
can afford to buy.

00:34:44.883 --> 00:34:48.887
It's about the price of what
we pay for HPC, you know,

00:34:48.887 --> 00:34:51.957
on sort of a national scale,
HPC efforts that, you know,

00:34:51.957 --> 00:34:54.392
DOE and NSF fund.

00:34:57.028 --> 00:34:59.731
That's a picture of a container
full of computers, so it's one

00:34:59.731 --> 00:35:03.301
or two computers that run a
hardware and software stack

00:35:03.301 --> 00:35:08.540
optimized for doing scientific
discovery over data and in 2004,

00:35:08.540 --> 00:35:12.811
when the industry did this,
it was at least giving you 10

00:35:12.811 --> 00:35:16.581
to 100 increase in the amount
of data you could build,

00:35:16.581 --> 00:35:19.818
statistical models,
and make discoveries over.

00:35:20.485 --> 00:35:25.156
One of the problems I've asked
myself, and I'll tell you a

00:35:25.156 --> 00:35:28.793
little bit about it, but not
much, is if we had the right

00:35:28.793 --> 00:35:35.867
Cyberpods and then, just as
there's a database is a way to,

00:35:35.867 --> 00:35:42.507
sort of, in one specialized
scaled up machine - databases

00:35:42.507 --> 00:35:44.909
drive a lot of science.

00:35:44.909 --> 00:35:49.214
You can ask is there a data pod,
that is, a data software stack

00:35:49.214 --> 00:35:53.251
over a cyberpod of data that
could drive data science and new

00:35:53.251 --> 00:35:55.153
discoveries as they come up?

00:35:55.153 --> 00:35:59.224
So, I think of cyberpods and
the software stack data pods.

00:35:59.224 --> 00:36:03.428
And so, if we could take all
the nation's around the world's

00:36:03.428 --> 00:36:08.333
cancer data and reanalyze it
every night, what would that

00:36:08.333 --> 00:36:11.002
allow us to make in terms of
discovery and treatment that

00:36:11.002 --> 00:36:12.270
we can't do today?

00:36:12.270 --> 00:36:15.140
And so, this is not absurd.

00:36:15.140 --> 00:36:23.114
In advertising, they may
advertise a billion individuals'

00:36:23.114 --> 00:36:29.954
response in history to do the
best advertising algorithms for

00:36:29.954 --> 00:36:32.857
the next day, you know,
it's done in other areas like

00:36:32.857 --> 00:36:35.060
advertising, like high
performance trading, so the

00:36:35.060 --> 00:36:37.696
question is, if those
disciplines can analyze all

00:36:37.696 --> 00:36:45.870
of the data in a field and have
a paradigm to make discoveries

00:36:45.870 --> 00:36:49.507
each day, why can't
we do that in science?

00:36:50.375 --> 00:36:52.944
And so, this is the picture
that I have to summarize

00:36:52.944 --> 00:36:54.079
the talk so far.

00:36:54.079 --> 00:36:58.483
We have this paradigm of where
we get really, really smart

00:36:58.483 --> 00:37:02.120
scientists, give them a small
amount of data and give them

00:37:02.120 --> 00:37:08.393
years to make a discovery and
they work over gigabytes or

00:37:08.393 --> 00:37:12.430
terabytes of data that is about
a watts of compute, and then,

00:37:12.430 --> 00:37:16.935
at the other end, you can ask
this question: is there the

00:37:16.935 --> 00:37:20.271
ability to work at the
megawatt level in cyber pods?

00:37:20.271 --> 00:37:22.974
You would probably build simpler
models, but you could redo

00:37:22.974 --> 00:37:23.975
them every day.

00:37:23.975 --> 00:37:27.679
You could reanalyze the
data every day and, again,

00:37:27.679 --> 00:37:30.582
I'm not arguing that one
side is better than others.

00:37:30.582 --> 00:37:35.053
I'm arguing that why not have a
portfolio of solutions in which

00:37:35.053 --> 00:37:38.389
you take whatever you can from
the whole spectrum of solution

00:37:38.389 --> 00:37:42.026
space and not ignore one
end of the solution space?

00:37:42.026 --> 00:37:45.196
And so, this is sort of the
division and there, you know,

00:37:45.196 --> 00:37:50.468
we have one person driving this
whole process that reanalyzes

00:37:50.468 --> 00:37:53.805
the data each day to make
discoveries with new data

00:37:53.805 --> 00:37:59.244
coming in - all the text data,
all the genomic data,

00:37:59.244 --> 00:38:01.980
all the associated
clinical data.

00:38:01.980 --> 00:38:06.317
So, I now want to talk
about Five Trends.

00:38:06.317 --> 00:38:08.820
I'm going to talk
about data commons.

00:38:08.820 --> 00:38:12.790
One of the terms we can think of
using for that right-hand side

00:38:12.790 --> 00:38:15.026
is a data commons.

00:38:15.026 --> 00:38:18.263
Almost no one talks about
data commons except for a

00:38:18.263 --> 00:38:20.131
few isolated colleagues.

00:38:20.131 --> 00:38:24.869
This is the Google Trends
for the term "data commons."

00:38:24.869 --> 00:38:28.706
If you compare that to
data mining or to big data,

00:38:28.706 --> 00:38:30.375
this is a noise term.

00:38:30.375 --> 00:38:31.810
That's buried in the noise.

00:38:31.810 --> 00:38:34.879
It doesn't even
arise above the noise.

00:38:36.347 --> 00:38:44.489
So, in the genomic sciences
the paradigm has broken because

00:38:44.489 --> 00:38:48.293
researchers who have a petabyte
of genomic data, any researcher

00:38:48.293 --> 00:38:51.429
who has a petabyte of genomic
can afford to have their own

00:38:51.429 --> 00:38:57.001
infrastructure, can afford
to hire the data scientists,

00:38:57.001 --> 00:39:00.138
the data engineers,
etc., to analyze it.

00:39:00.138 --> 00:39:04.242
So, we were forced, after
only five years of discussion,

00:39:04.242 --> 00:39:09.080
to create this other paradigm
in which we have a commons.

00:39:09.080 --> 00:39:13.985
And here, I'm defining a commons
where we co-locate data storage

00:39:13.985 --> 00:39:17.622
and computing infrastructure
commonly used tools for

00:39:17.622 --> 00:39:21.693
analyzing sharing data to create
a resource for the community.

00:39:21.693 --> 00:39:25.296
And so, the question is how do
we create commons at scale that

00:39:25.296 --> 00:39:28.333
can interoperate with
scientists' own infrastructure,

00:39:28.333 --> 00:39:31.035
that could peer with each other
and that can change the way

00:39:31.035 --> 00:39:34.405
we do data science at scale.

00:39:34.405 --> 00:39:37.942
And I'm not going
to talk about them.

00:39:37.942 --> 00:39:41.579
Our team, you know, we created
a not for profit called the

00:39:41.579 --> 00:39:44.482
Open Commons Consortium
to do this, has created a

00:39:44.482 --> 00:39:48.086
series of these for
the last several years.

00:39:48.086 --> 00:39:51.389
We created something called the
Open Science Data Cloud, which

00:39:51.389 --> 00:39:54.459
has about a petabyte of data,
and I referred to that before.

00:39:54.459 --> 00:39:57.962
That was supported by the MORE
Foundation for three years.

00:39:57.962 --> 00:40:01.799
We created something called the
Bionimbus Protected Data Cloud,

00:40:01.799 --> 00:40:04.269
another petabyte of
infrastructure which could hold

00:40:04.269 --> 00:40:09.507
genomes and clinical data
from large cancer projects

00:40:09.507 --> 00:40:11.242
funded by the NCI.

00:40:11.242 --> 00:40:14.312
We're building, and it's in beta
testing, and so, if you guys are

00:40:14.312 --> 00:40:18.449
bored and don't have anything to
do, you could beta test this.

00:40:18.449 --> 00:40:24.022
The NCI Genomic Data Commons is
an infrastructure that's going

00:40:24.022 --> 00:40:28.126
to hold research data from all
National Cancer Institute funded

00:40:28.126 --> 00:40:33.031
projects and any NCI funded
researcher and they, as part of

00:40:33.031 --> 00:40:37.035
their funding, are required to
put their data into a commons

00:40:37.035 --> 00:40:39.637
and it's the NCI
genomic data commons.

00:40:39.637 --> 00:40:43.041
It's in beta now, and be
released in a one-level release

00:40:43.041 --> 00:40:45.877
in November and another
release in May for general use.

00:40:45.877 --> 00:40:53.751
And then, the NOAA also has a
commons for all their data and

00:40:53.751 --> 00:40:54.786
we're building one of them.

00:40:54.786 --> 00:40:58.389
And then Amazon,
Google, IBM, Microsoft

00:40:58.389 --> 00:41:00.425
are also building commons.

00:41:00.425 --> 00:41:03.094
Ours is focused on the
research community.

00:41:03.094 --> 00:41:05.330
This is a picture of the
Genomic Data Commons.

00:41:05.330 --> 00:41:08.166
It has, today, two
petabytes growing to five

00:41:08.166 --> 00:41:09.867
petabytes of data.

00:41:09.867 --> 00:41:14.605
We analyze all that data as a
common analysis, the data that

00:41:14.605 --> 00:41:18.543
comes in has, by and large,
been analyzed either by single

00:41:18.543 --> 00:41:21.279
research groups or by the
centers that put them together,

00:41:21.279 --> 00:41:24.816
so this is a reanalysis of all
the data and we're designing a

00:41:24.816 --> 00:41:30.288
system that could reanalyze
all NCI funded data.

00:41:30.288 --> 00:41:33.257
Every few months, we like to
scale up the infrastructure so

00:41:33.257 --> 00:41:36.060
we could do it
even faster than that.

00:41:36.060 --> 00:41:38.796
We downloaded the two petabytes
of data and processed it

00:41:38.796 --> 00:41:39.530
in real time.

00:41:39.530 --> 00:41:44.736
QC redid the alignment and
etcetera and this is a picture

00:41:44.736 --> 00:41:46.671
from a part of a day.

00:41:46.671 --> 00:41:51.476
Each of those lines is a file.

00:41:51.476 --> 00:41:56.114
The time is horizontal and it's
the length of time it took us to

00:41:56.114 --> 00:42:00.785
process it from files that are
almost a terabyte in size that

00:42:00.785 --> 00:42:04.255
we downloaded, two files that
are megabytes and gigabytes.

00:42:04.255 --> 00:42:07.225
I think it's a half
a million files.

00:42:07.225 --> 00:42:12.096
We brought it in at 8 or 9
gigabytes per second for weeks

00:42:12.096 --> 00:42:13.765
to analyze it.

00:42:18.336 --> 00:42:22.140
I like to do things that are
simple, so once you have data

00:42:22.140 --> 00:42:24.709
that no one else has
done you could do pretty

00:42:24.709 --> 00:42:26.044
simple statistical models.

00:42:26.044 --> 00:42:31.616
So, this is a statistical model
we built where the gray are

00:42:31.616 --> 00:42:34.886
adenocarcinoma, it's a
particular type of lung cancer.

00:42:34.886 --> 00:42:37.522
The purple are
squamous cell carcinoma.

00:42:37.522 --> 00:42:40.925
We took the RNA seek data.

00:42:40.925 --> 00:42:44.562
We did principle components
analysis in about 400 dimensions

00:42:44.562 --> 00:42:48.900
and we just built some simple
statistical models and the

00:42:48.900 --> 00:42:51.369
green are things that
seem to be misclassified

00:42:51.369 --> 00:42:52.837
under this analysis.

00:42:52.837 --> 00:42:55.306
It's about 10% in there.

00:42:55.306 --> 00:42:58.042
There's a drug, Avastin, that
works for one that doesn't work

00:42:58.042 --> 00:42:58.776
for the other.

00:42:58.776 --> 00:43:00.845
It actually shortens
your life in about half

00:43:00.845 --> 00:43:02.246
if it's misapplied.

00:43:02.246 --> 00:43:04.882
So, this is an example of a
relatively simple statistical

00:43:04.882 --> 00:43:08.186
model that could only be done
when you have enough data that

00:43:08.186 --> 00:43:12.523
has a huge impact, that's
relatively simple to do as long

00:43:12.523 --> 00:43:14.625
as you have access to the
data and could do statistical

00:43:14.625 --> 00:43:16.694
analysis at scale.

00:43:18.196 --> 00:43:21.032
I want to talk about
the second trend.

00:43:21.766 --> 00:43:25.436
This is really analytics of
things, people, and places.

00:43:25.436 --> 00:43:31.275
We have urban sensors that
we're putting into Chicago.

00:43:31.275 --> 00:43:33.177
There are a number of
instrumenting cities.

00:43:33.177 --> 00:43:36.180
This is the idea that to
understand urban science,

00:43:36.180 --> 00:43:39.817
let's instrument a city, that's
a conceptual version because the

00:43:39.817 --> 00:43:44.489
actual sensors in Chicago are
much uglier, but that's what

00:43:44.489 --> 00:43:47.558
they thought it would look like
before they actually built it.

00:43:47.558 --> 00:43:50.428
They're actually putting sensors
into all sorts of things, so you

00:43:50.428 --> 00:43:54.966
could put sensors into waste
bins and other things in cities,

00:43:54.966 --> 00:43:58.102
so, cities are going to be
instrumented and produce lots of

00:43:58.102 --> 00:44:01.572
data that's going to change how
we think about urban science.

00:44:01.572 --> 00:44:05.743
I think most people have this,
but there's also people are

00:44:05.743 --> 00:44:08.346
going to be producing lots of
data that will change how we

00:44:08.346 --> 00:44:12.750
think about social science and
health science, and places are

00:44:12.750 --> 00:44:14.785
going to generate data.

00:44:15.987 --> 00:44:21.359
Many cars have phones or
devices that emit probes.

00:44:21.359 --> 00:44:27.932
The purple is where people brake
and you could observe that the

00:44:27.932 --> 00:44:31.002
cars that don't brake
in the right place can't

00:44:31.002 --> 00:44:32.069
navigate the curve.

00:44:32.069 --> 00:44:36.974
So, I use the term kouros
space, Greek kouros for place,

00:44:36.974 --> 00:44:39.544
to be the analytics
associated with place.

00:44:39.544 --> 00:44:45.416
So, as you have things giving
off data, people giving off

00:44:45.416 --> 00:44:50.488
data, you can annotate space
in interesting ways and here we

00:44:50.488 --> 00:44:56.627
could, if we see a car not going
slow enough, you could inform

00:44:56.627 --> 00:45:00.264
the car, inform the driver, that
if they don't slow down they're

00:45:00.264 --> 00:45:01.766
not going to be
able to navigate that.

00:45:01.766 --> 00:45:06.270
And this is a very simple model
that is clear to how to build

00:45:06.270 --> 00:45:09.006
once you have enough data.

00:45:09.006 --> 00:45:15.646
My third trend is we're not
doing sometimes very deep

00:45:15.646 --> 00:45:19.750
computer science for big data
and I'd like just to remind

00:45:19.750 --> 00:45:25.089
people, we've talked about the
software stack that does new

00:45:25.089 --> 00:45:27.525
programming paradigms,
I haven't talked about

00:45:27.525 --> 00:45:28.526
the networking stack.

00:45:28.526 --> 00:45:30.628
The data centers have
new types of networks.

00:45:30.628 --> 00:45:34.398
But, I do want to mention that
one of the most powerful things

00:45:34.398 --> 00:45:38.002
we have in computer science
is the ability to use languages.

00:45:38.002 --> 00:45:42.106
So, right now, we think of
computer scientists as heroes

00:45:42.106 --> 00:45:46.444
that can do whatever they want
with no discipline and as long

00:45:46.444 --> 00:45:49.113
as they get an answer
out we're happy.

00:45:49.113 --> 00:45:51.916
But, why not give them tools and
force them to use the tools?

00:45:51.916 --> 00:45:55.086
For example, there are
languages for graphics.

00:45:55.086 --> 00:45:59.690
My colleague, Leland Wilkerson,
wrote a very influential book

00:45:59.690 --> 00:46:02.493
called The Grammar of Graphics,
so that when we did

00:46:02.493 --> 00:46:08.666
visualization in exploratory
data systems we could really do

00:46:08.666 --> 00:46:12.803
that with grammar so that we can
manipulate the language that

00:46:12.803 --> 00:46:14.472
describes the graphics
instead of always

00:46:14.472 --> 00:46:15.940
manipulating the graphics.

00:46:15.940 --> 00:46:20.044
I've spent about 15 years
of my life building models for

00:46:20.044 --> 00:46:24.515
analytics so that, at scale,
when we have lots of models

00:46:24.515 --> 00:46:28.719
like that, we work with
the models using a language.

00:46:28.719 --> 00:46:30.988
It was first a predictive model
markup language, now the

00:46:30.988 --> 00:46:34.091
portable format for analytics,
and this allows you to scale

00:46:34.091 --> 00:46:37.461
up to cyberpods and data
centers, how you build

00:46:37.461 --> 00:46:38.562
analytics at scale.

00:46:38.562 --> 00:46:44.602
And if you think about
visualization, I pull out D-3,

00:46:44.602 --> 00:46:47.505
but there are others that
allow you to describe what

00:46:47.505 --> 00:46:51.175
you want to do, not how
you want to visualize.

00:46:51.175 --> 00:46:55.946
The fourth trend is, you know,
there are policies and they're

00:46:55.946 --> 00:46:59.750
going to create other policies
that are going to encourage

00:46:59.750 --> 00:47:01.952
people to make data available.

00:47:01.952 --> 00:47:07.391
The government is now asked to
make data available under the

00:47:07.391 --> 00:47:13.497
Exec Order 13642 from May '13,
and the guidance from OMB.

00:47:13.497 --> 00:47:18.469
That led to the NOAA,
which was the first agency to

00:47:18.469 --> 00:47:22.440
operationalize this in terms of
a CRADA, that is a cooperative

00:47:22.440 --> 00:47:26.977
agreement that allowed our
consortium and IBM, Microsoft,

00:47:26.977 --> 00:47:36.020
and Google to partner with NOAA
to have access to any of the

00:47:36.020 --> 00:47:39.156
20 petabytes to make it
available to, in our case

00:47:39.156 --> 00:47:42.927
researchers, to the other
companies to mainly companies,

00:47:42.927 --> 00:47:46.163
but that was driven
by the OMB initiative.

00:47:46.163 --> 00:47:49.700
There's also initiatives
for preservation and others,

00:47:49.700 --> 00:47:53.604
thoughts about reproducibility,
so there's a C change coming and

00:47:53.604 --> 00:47:56.307
we don't really have the
infrastructure to do that.

00:47:56.307 --> 00:47:58.909
And then, I'm going to talk
about the last trend and then

00:47:58.909 --> 00:48:01.379
leave time for Q&amp;A...

00:48:01.379 --> 00:48:03.948
Is that clock right?

00:48:03.948 --> 00:48:04.715
Okay, good.

00:48:04.715 --> 00:48:07.118
A lot of times, they put
me in places where the clocks

00:48:07.118 --> 00:48:10.287
are wrong, just to challenge me.

00:48:14.792 --> 00:48:18.829
Early in my career, I did math
and the nice thing about math

00:48:18.829 --> 00:48:21.832
that doesn't apply to anyone.

00:48:21.832 --> 00:48:25.970
The last little bit of my career
I'm a little more interested in,

00:48:25.970 --> 00:48:30.374
as you do things like data
science, can it apply to anyone?

00:48:30.374 --> 00:48:34.245
And so, I'm going to
talk about what I call

00:48:34.245 --> 00:48:36.680
translational data science.

00:48:37.381 --> 00:48:43.854
So, the term "translational" was
created a number of years ago

00:48:43.854 --> 00:48:50.995
around this notion that there
was a big lag between how long

00:48:50.995 --> 00:48:56.033
it took a basic discovery in
biology or medicine to go from

00:48:56.033 --> 00:48:58.869
the lab to the patient.

00:48:58.869 --> 00:49:07.378
And so, if we think of this as
informatics in general, we have

00:49:07.378 --> 00:49:10.047
bioinformatics at the
molecular and cellular level,

00:49:10.047 --> 00:49:12.983
imaging informatics,
clinical informatics,

00:49:12.983 --> 00:49:15.719
public health informatics,
but we have translational in

00:49:15.719 --> 00:49:17.021
each of those things.

00:49:17.021 --> 00:49:21.892
And so, translational
informatics is something that

00:49:21.892 --> 00:49:24.428
people have been
struggling with for 10 years.

00:49:24.428 --> 00:49:28.132
So, one of the things that's
been motivating us in our center

00:49:28.132 --> 00:49:30.868
is what you might call
translational data science;

00:49:30.868 --> 00:49:34.171
that is, if you don't just think
about the discovery but you

00:49:34.171 --> 00:49:37.007
think about how to accelerate
the impact of that discovery on

00:49:37.007 --> 00:49:45.249
populations or patients or
society, there's this notion

00:49:45.249 --> 00:49:48.252
that we spend all our time
thinking about the hardware,

00:49:48.252 --> 00:49:55.526
software, and modeling but most
of the translational effort is

00:49:55.526 --> 00:49:59.730
setting up the infrastructure,
getting the data on one side and

00:49:59.730 --> 00:50:02.166
then deploying the model
and checking it and making

00:50:02.166 --> 00:50:03.534
it actionable on the other.

00:50:03.534 --> 00:50:06.270
And the actual modeling
is a very small component.

00:50:06.270 --> 00:50:11.242
So, if you want to optimize the
translational impact you have

00:50:11.242 --> 00:50:13.444
to have things like
languages for deployment.

00:50:13.444 --> 00:50:16.313
That's why we created a
portable format for analytics.

00:50:16.313 --> 00:50:21.051
You have to create standard
systems like cyberpods to take

00:50:21.051 --> 00:50:24.021
care of the data so that you
can impact the whole thing.

00:50:24.021 --> 00:50:26.724
So, we think of the data
engineering, data science,

00:50:26.724 --> 00:50:30.895
and applications, in this case
to genomics analysis of medical

00:50:30.895 --> 00:50:34.031
records, etc., where we could
get discoveries, diagnosis,

00:50:34.031 --> 00:50:36.734
and therapeutics out as
being sort of a way to do

00:50:36.734 --> 00:50:38.769
translational data science.

00:50:41.839 --> 00:50:44.909
This is some example of that
where we're trying to take large

00:50:44.909 --> 00:50:48.212
amounts of environmental,
electronic medical records and

00:50:48.212 --> 00:50:51.181
try to understand how
we can make impacts.

00:50:51.181 --> 00:50:54.818
And this is the distribution
of two diseases.

00:50:54.818 --> 00:50:57.087
One clearly relates to
the physical environment.

00:50:57.087 --> 00:50:59.056
That turned out - we
just analyzed the data.

00:50:59.056 --> 00:51:00.991
It was about 100 million
medical records.

00:51:00.991 --> 00:51:03.160
The top turns out to
be a well known disease.

00:51:03.160 --> 00:51:07.631
And we rank-ordered about
1,000 diseases by ICD 9 codes

00:51:07.631 --> 00:51:11.335
from those that have most
geospatial variation to those

00:51:11.335 --> 00:51:12.069
that have least.

00:51:12.069 --> 00:51:15.039
And then we were doing a
simple classifier to ask it,

00:51:15.039 --> 00:51:18.242
is the geospatial variation
due to environmental,

00:51:18.242 --> 00:51:23.781
socioeconomic, structure,
like payer-provider, or other?

00:51:23.781 --> 00:51:26.984
And the top is something
that's clearly physical when

00:51:26.984 --> 00:51:27.785
you look at it.

00:51:27.785 --> 00:51:30.387
The middle, it's not so obvious
what that is, but that's

00:51:30.387 --> 00:51:32.122
really socioeconomic driven.

00:51:32.122 --> 00:51:35.192
And then you can see things that
are payer-provider because they

00:51:35.192 --> 00:51:40.397
split very much on
the jurisdictional lines.

00:51:40.397 --> 00:51:44.435
So, I want to end
with five challenges.

00:51:46.270 --> 00:51:49.573
I think the one that's probably
most to my hard, and maybe the

00:51:49.573 --> 00:51:54.311
hardest is this sort of way that
Phil Anderson, a Nobel Prize

00:51:54.311 --> 00:51:56.213
winning physicist
asked the question.

00:51:56.213 --> 00:52:01.385
He summarized his work in
basically solid state physics as

00:52:01.385 --> 00:52:07.057
really being concerned with the
question is at scale, you know,

00:52:07.057 --> 00:52:11.795
as you add more and more things,
are there new emergent behavior

00:52:11.795 --> 00:52:12.763
that's really different.

00:52:12.763 --> 00:52:17.401
And there's this famous
Science article in '72 called

00:52:17.401 --> 00:52:18.802
"Is More Different?"

00:52:18.802 --> 00:52:21.672
And I think, to me, this is
the fundamental question.

00:52:21.672 --> 00:52:26.443
A lot of statisticians believe,
and are certainly right at some

00:52:26.443 --> 00:52:30.714
level, that there is no such
thing as big data, there is only

00:52:30.714 --> 00:52:32.816
good and bad statistics.

00:52:32.816 --> 00:52:36.854
On the other hand, at a
phenomenological level, you sort

00:52:36.854 --> 00:52:41.225
of believe as you go to scale
certain phenomena appear that

00:52:41.225 --> 00:52:43.327
just don't appear and that
certainly is the case in solid

00:52:43.327 --> 00:52:46.597
state physics and, to me, the
question in data science is -

00:52:46.597 --> 00:52:49.867
one of the outstanding questions
is - what discoveries can we

00:52:49.867 --> 00:52:53.470
make in data science that show
emerging properties that just

00:52:53.470 --> 00:52:55.606
aren't there at smaller scale?

00:52:56.940 --> 00:53:01.145
The second challenge I've
alluded to sometimes is one

00:53:01.145 --> 00:53:01.979
I've been working on.

00:53:01.979 --> 00:53:06.350
It's one of the reasons we
created the Commons for Genomic

00:53:06.350 --> 00:53:14.024
and Clinical Data is - and doing
a million genomes was part of

00:53:14.024 --> 00:53:20.197
the Presidential Precisional
Medicine Initiative -

00:53:20.197 --> 00:53:23.367
at $1,000, it's roughly a
billion dollars of sequencing,

00:53:23.367 --> 00:53:26.637
but if there's about a
terabyte of data as we get more

00:53:26.637 --> 00:53:30.741
data for patients, a million
terabytes is a thousand

00:53:30.741 --> 00:53:33.877
petabytes is an exabyte,
that's something that is

00:53:33.877 --> 00:53:37.081
outside of what we could
easily do in science today.

00:53:37.081 --> 00:53:42.419
But, it would probably
fundamentally change because

00:53:42.419 --> 00:53:46.990
cancer and other diseases
are about combinations

00:53:46.990 --> 00:53:47.925
of rare variance.

00:53:47.925 --> 00:53:51.295
We don't have the statistical
power, we thought we did,
00:53:51.295,00:53:51.228
but every time we think we do,
we're sort of surprised by the

00:53:57.234 --> 00:54:00.637
complexity of cancer, but a
million - you know, you hope

00:54:00.637 --> 00:54:03.640
that with a million genomes, an
exabyte of data, that you would

00:54:03.640 --> 00:54:04.608
have an understanding.

00:54:04.608 --> 00:54:08.078
But understanding statistics
at that level is really

00:54:08.078 --> 00:54:09.947
a big challenge.

00:54:10.681 --> 00:54:14.284
The other one I'm going to say
is how do we create tools for

00:54:14.284 --> 00:54:22.326
research communities, data pods,
and pod bases that allow us to

00:54:22.326 --> 00:54:25.896
easily give open source
infrastructure that's simple and

00:54:25.896 --> 00:54:29.666
easy to manage to researchers
so they have instruments for

00:54:29.666 --> 00:54:35.539
discovery at the scale that
data science sometimes asks.

00:54:37.774 --> 00:54:41.545
I actually have 10 challenges,
but I only have time for 5,

00:54:41.545 --> 00:54:46.016
so, as we get to challenge 4
and 5, sometimes they're above

00:54:46.016 --> 00:54:47.251
and below the line.

00:54:47.251 --> 00:54:49.653
I think this is an
interesting problem.

00:54:49.653 --> 00:54:52.990
We know how to deal with
a billion feature vectors.

00:54:52.990 --> 00:54:55.325
We know how to deal
with a million dimensions.

00:54:55.325 --> 00:54:59.396
I don't think we have a very
good understanding of how we can

00:54:59.396 --> 00:55:03.500
semi automate the production of
a billion statistical models and

00:55:03.500 --> 00:55:05.469
make sure that we can
validate them and understand.

00:55:05.469 --> 00:55:08.939
So, one of the things I'm
interested in is how do we scale

00:55:08.939 --> 00:55:11.475
up our ability to work
with very, very large

00:55:11.475 --> 00:55:12.676
numbers of models.

00:55:12.676 --> 00:55:17.514
And, again, if you think back
on some things I've said about

00:55:17.514 --> 00:55:20.984
languages, infrastructure,
pods, it's really - this is

00:55:20.984 --> 00:55:22.786
parts of it.

00:55:23.720 --> 00:55:26.523
And the last one, we
have something called HCI,

00:55:26.523 --> 00:55:28.392
Human Computer Interaction.

00:55:28.392 --> 00:55:33.197
I think it might be interesting
to imagine what a human data

00:55:33.197 --> 00:55:34.898
science interaction is.

00:55:34.898 --> 00:55:38.268
Initially, it was
the data science and

00:55:38.268 --> 00:55:40.737
statistician doing everything.

00:55:40.737 --> 00:55:43.740
There was a period of time when
the statistician did nothing.

00:55:43.740 --> 00:55:47.444
I actually think there's sort of
a nice interesting middle ground

00:55:47.444 --> 00:55:51.748
where the data scientist steers
these very large systems and

00:55:51.748 --> 00:55:53.817
it's sort of a good time.

00:55:53.817 --> 00:55:56.687
People with mechanical turk,
they have a lot of things,

00:55:56.687 --> 00:56:01.024
but, how can we bring the
human back into this loop to

00:56:01.024 --> 00:56:01.959
scale things up?

00:56:01.959 --> 00:56:09.333
And so, every year or so, I post
a blog so I'm due for one,

00:56:09.333 --> 00:56:14.371
so, I'm going to put the
9 or 10 challenges up there

00:56:14.371 --> 00:56:15.472
and some references.

00:56:15.472 --> 00:56:18.809
But, that's all I have
to say for today.

00:56:18.809 --> 00:56:21.879
(Applause)

